+//! `UploadPart`'s **staging half** (#657, its first slice): reserve one in-flight slot, stream
+//! the part body into chunks, register each chunk as an owned `sidx:` entry **before** any of its
+//! fragments is written, write the fragments under a bounded authorization deadline, and renew
+//! the slot and the owned leases for as long as the request is live (proposal 0016 decision 5,
+//! `0016:1676-1847`, and its *Part slot reserve* and *Part intent* rows, `0016:658-659`). What
+//! [`stage_part`] hands back, a [`StagedAttempt`], is everything the part commit builds its batch
+//! (or its compensation) from.
+//!
+//! | Batch | Preconditions | Puts |
+//! |---|---|---|
+//! | reserve | `require(mpu:<id> == Open@E)` (the bytes read), `require_absent(slot:<id>:<k>)` | the slot record |
+//! | intent, one per chunk | `require(mpu:<id> == Open@E)`, `require_absent(desired:dserver:<S>)` for every `S` in the placement, `require(slot:<id>:<k> == prior)`, `require_absent(sidx:<id>:<n>:<chunk>)` | the owned entry, and the slot with its lease moved forward |
+//! | renewal | `require(mpu:<id> == Open@E)`, `require(slot:<id>:<k> == prior)`, `require(sidx: == prior)` for every owned entry | the slot and every owned entry, each lease moved forward |
+//!
+//! Nothing here writes `part:` or `psum:`, releases a slot or deletes an owned entry: the part
+//! commit and its compensation are the rest of #657. No production path calls this module yet
+//! (the S3 verb is #669).
+//!
+//! # Order, and the drain fence
+//!
+//! A chunk's fragments are written only once its owned entry has been **observed durable** — a
+//! `Committed` answer to its intent, or a re-read finding the entry's exact bytes. So no staged
+//! byte exists that no `sidx:` record names (`0016:1530-1531`). The entry records the placement
+//! the fragments go to, planned against the topology **minus every server with a drain record**
+//! ([`Topology::excluding`]), and the intent `require_absent`s the drain record of each server it
+//! names. An intent that loses to a drain recorded after it planned re-plans; it never refuses
+//! the part for it (`0016:659`).
+//!
+//! Every fragment write carries the deadline `t_auth + W_write`, `t_auth` being the clock reading
+//! taken before its chunk's intent was committed, and the chunk store refuses a write that would
+//! land after it (#638). The wait for those writes is bounded at the same instant, fail-closed
+//! (`0016:1555-1557`): writes still pending once their deadline has passed are abandoned, since
+//! no store may apply them any more, and staging stops ([`Stop::WriteDeadlineElapsed`]).
+//!
+//! # Renewal
+//!
+//! Half a lease TTL after the last renewal (or the reservation), one batch moves every lease of
+//! the attempt forward: the slot **and** every owned entry. It runs for the whole request: before
+//! each intent, before each body read and before each fragment write, **and while one is
+//! pending**. Core spawns nothing (ADR-0009), so each pending body read and fragment write is
+//! raced against the caller's [`StageRuntime::wait`] for the next renewal instant; when the wait
+//! wins, the renewal is sent and the pending read or write is resumed, never dropped or re-sent.
+//! A renewal is never sent while this attempt's own intent batch is in flight: both rewrite the
+//! slot under `require(slot == prior)`, so they would only fence each other out.
+//!
+//! A renewal that finds a lease of its own already lapsed **refuses and writes nothing**
+//! (`0016:1843-1847`, the rule `write::lease_write_chunk` applies to an ordinary streaming
+//! write): a lapsed lease is dead authority. Every lease a batch here writes is strictly later
+//! than the slot lease it replaces, so no lease moves backwards and every batch puts a slot value
+//! no earlier batch of the attempt put.
+//!
+//! # A commit that did not say `Committed`
+//!
+//! The store's report is never guessed at. After `Conflict` or a [`CommitUnknownResult`], the
+//! batch's own record is read back — the slot for a reserve or a renewal, the owned entry for an
+//! intent — and only its exact bytes, or this attempt's id in the slot, prove it landed. Absence
+//! settles an outcome only when the store said it can no longer land (`may_still_commit:
+//! false`). With `may_still_commit: true` the doubt is **kept**: every later batch of the
+//! attempt requires the slot (or, for a reserve, the slot's absence) at the bytes the doubtful
+//! batch also requires, so at most one of them lands, and a loop that runs out of rounds returns
+//! [`StageError::InDoubt`] carrying the doubt, as `abort::abort_session` does. A read that finds
+//! the session moved, or the slot no longer this attempt's, clears it: every batch requires both.
+//!
+//! # Bounds
+//!
+//! | Loop | Most commits |
+//! |---|---|
+//! | reserve, probing free indices | `max_inflight_parts`; then `SlowDown` |
+//! | reserve, retrying the index a doubtful batch may still install | [`RESERVE_AMBIGUOUS_ROUNDS`]; then `SlowDown` |
+//! | intent, per chunk (re-plans included) | [`INTENT_ROUNDS`] |
+//! | renewal, per renewal | [`RENEWAL_ROUNDS`] |
+//! | chunks, per part | `max_part_chunks`; a longer body stops staging ([`StagedAttempt::chunk_bound_exceeded`]) |
+//!
+//! # One clock, and no runtime (ADR-0009)
+//!
+//! Every time this module reads comes from the caller's [`StageRuntime::now_millis`]: the
+//! reservation stamp, every lease, every lapse check and every write deadline. That clock owns
+//! the staging lease lifecycle, so it must be the one the reaper and GC judge those leases by (the
+//! deployment wall clock in production; madsim's under DST). The renewal timer
+//! ([`StageRuntime::wait`]) must count the same time. Chunk ids come from the caller too.
+//!
+//! The tests drive these batches over a real store with every interleaving forced by a scripted
+//! wrapper (`crates/core/tests/multipart_upload_part_stage.rs`). Seeded DST coverage of the races
+//! (an intent against a drain request, a renewal against a fence) is deferred: #660, #665.
+
